        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Counter-Reformation</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Spanish Netherlands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as missions to reconvert; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> through missionary orders</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">earlier Catholic reform from the late 15th century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> 1545–1563; enforcement of its decrees and the wars of religion to 1648, the date most often given for its end, though this is controversial; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands until the Patent of Toleration of 1781</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>, the first pope of the Counter-Reformation; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo">Carlo Borromeo</a>, reforming Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola">Ignatius of Loyola</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila">Teresa of Ávila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross">John of the Cross</a>, Spanish mystics; Philip Neri, founder of the Oratorians; Francis de Sales; Alexander Farnese, who reconquered the southern Netherlands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">a reformed clergy, new religious orders and a restated doctrine; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> drastically diminished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, less so in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>; Protestants expelled, forced to convert or fled as refugees; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style in art</span></div>
