    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Counter-Reformation</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="Martin Luther" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Martin Luther <span class="kid-tale-cr">Lucas Cranach the Elder, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 500 years ago, Martin Luther wrote his 95 Theses, a list of complaints against the way the Catholic Church raised money.</p><p>Soon many people broke away from the Catholic Church and became Protestants.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/counter-reformation.jpg" alt="Counter-Reformation" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Counter-Reformation <span class="kid-tale-cr">Bartolomé Esteban Murillo, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Catholic Church answered with a movement of its own, called the Counter-Reformation.</p><p>Some people in the Church had wanted change for a long time, because many priests were poorly trained.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-trent.jpg" alt="Council of Trent" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Council of Trent <span class="kid-tale-cr">Laurom, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>From 1545 to 1563, church leaders met again and again at the Council of Trent.</p><p>They kept the old Catholic beliefs and set up schools to train priests better.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola.jpg" alt="Ignatius of Loyola" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Ignatius of Loyola <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>New religious orders led the change, above all the Jesuits, who followed Ignatius of Loyola.</p><p>Jesuits travelled as missionaries to the Americas and Asia to spread the Catholic faith.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="Galileo Galilei" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Galileo Galilei <span class="kid-tale-cr">Justus Sustermans, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Catholic rulers also fought wars, and many Protestants were forced to leave their homes or change their faith.</p><p>Church leaders turned against new science too: in 1633 the scientist Galileo Galilei was kept under arrest in his house.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini.jpg" alt="Gian Lorenzo Bernini" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Gian Lorenzo Bernini <span class="kid-tale-cr">Gian Lorenzo Bernini, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>It changed art as well: artists like Bernini helped to create a rich new style called the Baroque.</p><p>The Counter-Reformation shaped Catholic worship for centuries.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> (1545–1563) rejected all compromise with the Protestants, restating Catholic doctrine on grace and works, the seven sacraments and the veneration of saints, while reforming the training and discipline of priests and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</li><li>New orders led the revival, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, recognised in 1540, who organised along military lines, served the pope and carried missions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and Asia.</li><li>By politics, war and discriminatory law the Counter-Reformation won back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, while many Protestants were expelled, forced to convert or fled.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the Holy Office objected to the “buffoons, drunken Germans, dwarfs” in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-veronese">Paolo Veronese</a>’s huge Last Supper, he was told to change the painting within three months; he changed only its title, to The Feast in the House of Levi, and no more was said.</p></aside>
    </section>

